{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Phase 2 final report\n\nProblem: macOS pytest basetemp cleanup failed after successful snapshot tests because read-only run_snapshots trees survived teardown.\n\nProven root cause: production snapshot publication intentionally applies 0444/0555 permissions, while cleanup was owned only by the module-local fixture in tests/unit/workflow/test_snapshot.py. Snapshot producers in any other module therefore left a non-removable .rebar/run_snapshots/<sha> below pytest basetemp. Runtime reproduction showed ordinary shutil.rmtree raising PermissionError on that exact residual tree, while snapshot._rmtree_writable removed the same tree.\n\nTest proof: tests/unit/test_ci_snapshot_fixture_cleanup.py:22 creates a real git repository from a second module, publishes a real snapshot, and proves the file is non-writable. The nested-pytest oracle at line 46 was collected and executed, nested pytest exited 0, and the outer ordinary shutil.rmtree was legitimately RED with PermissionError at the residual .rebar/run_snapshots/<sha>. The oracle was held out from the blind fixer. Against the shared teardown it is GREEN. Exact-defect mutation removed the shared fixture and restored the old module-local-only cleanup; the final strengthened oracle returned to the same PermissionError RED after its adjacent-content assertion passed. The fix was then restored and the oracle returned GREEN.\n\nFix: tests/conftest.py:342 now owns an autouse teardown that searches only below each test-owned tmp_path for exact .rebar/run_snapshots roots and reuses snapshot._rmtree_writable. The superseded module-local fixture and unused Iterator import were removed from tests/unit/workflow/test_snapshot.py. Production snapshot permissions are unchanged.\n\nScope and safety: this restores the existing cleanup invariant at the shared pytest lifecycle boundary; it adds no production behavior, external dependency, or external call. The existing read-only assertions remain intact and no assertion was weakened. The cross-module oracle also leaves adjacent.txt beside the subject tmp_path and proves it survives shared teardown before ordinary basetemp removal, so unrelated temp content is not removed or chmodded.\n\nSibling/parity sweep: snapshot_at_ref producers were inventoried across tests/unit/workflow/test_snapshot.py, tests/unit/workflow/test_prior_art_hardening.py, and the new cross-module oracle. Prior-art hardening self-cleans in-body and remains green; the shared fixture covers current and future tmp_path-backed producers. CLI, MCP/API, public-library facade, automated runtime callers, and dual-direction parity are N/A because this is a pytest harness teardown lifecycle, not a production operation. No missing parallel surface remains.\n\nVerification: exact held-out GREEN passed; focused snapshot/cleanup/repository-isolation suite passed 30 tests; make lint passed; make typecheck passed with 555 source files; git diff --check passed. Refactoring litmus passes because the oracle asserts observable permissions, nested subprocess exit, adjacent-file preservation, and ordinary directory removal rather than fixture names or source structure.\n\nResidual risk and prevention: none observed beyond this repaired ownership seam.\n\nReplication: the original end-to-end shape now succeeds locally: nested pytest creates and validates a read-only snapshot, exits 0, shared teardown removes the exact cache root, and plain shutil.rmtree removes the explicit basetemp."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787030172822244001,"uuid":"990f1dc7-ce23-46c9-a8db-58973fac7cfe"}